  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nEarly this morning, the UP remains on the northern periphery of \nbroader midlevel ridging over the central CONUS, with a subtle \nshortwave rippling through, while a deepening midlevel low spins \nover Nevada. At the surface, low pressure is centered over central \nWI, and associated WAA into the UP has been able to touch off a \nround of widespread showers and storms. These are moving through the \neastern half of the UP, and should largely be out of the area by \nsunrise. MRMS indicates a widespread 0.25-.50in of rain has fallen \nso far tonight, with higher embedded totals as high as 0.7-0.9in. \nThis aligns well with available surface observations. Rainfall rates \nover the area at this time are beginning to fall back, so these \nhigher-end amounts are not looking so likely in the eastern UP, but \ntotals up to a half-inch are possible. Will not rule out some \nlingering light rainfall into the morning, both in the east as the \nsystem slowly pulls away, and over the far west and north-central \nwith upsloping NNE flow. Temperatures currently range in the upper \n40s to lower 50s for most, and should only fall back a couple \ndegrees further the rest of tonight. Under cloudy skies and with a \ncooler airmass gradually sinking in, temperatures only rise as far \nas the mid and upper 50s across most of the UP. However, the south-\ncentral UP should see highs rising into the lower to mid 60s. \n\nRidging slowly recovers Wednesday, allowing for a return to dry and \ngenerally quiet weather through at least early Thursday. It will be \notherwise gloomy as skies stay cloudy for most of the day, though \nmore breaks in the clouds are possible into Wednesday night. \nTemperatures peak in the 50s Wednesday afternoon, then plummet into \nthe lower 30s for most of the UP overnight. The interior-western UP \nturns even colder, especially if skies can clear partially; expect \nlows to range in the mid/upper 20s there. Temperatures peak in the \nmid/upper 50s Thursday, then dry weather finally gives way to \nrain chances by the evening ahead of a couple approaching systems. \n\nBy Thursday, the Nevada low will have moved over the Rockies, with a \nsurface low finally closing off and ejecting out into the Plains by \nthe afternoon. Meanwhile, a Clipper wave will be dropping out of \nAlberta. Guidance remains in agreement that this clipper will phase \nwith the Colorado low right around 12Z Friday over the Great Lakes, \nkicking off a quick round of rainfall Thursday evening and night. \nThis may be followed by lingering lake effect rain Friday as 850mb \ntemperatures fall below 0C, with lake surface temperatures still in \nthe lower teens. Meanwhile, surface low pressure rapidly deepens \nover the Lower Great Lakes, setting up a very tight pressure \ngradient over the Upper Midwest that results in strengthening \nnortherly winds. Multiple ensembles hint at strongly elevated wind \npotential during Friday. While the jury's still out on whether or \nnot this pattern will generate storm-force winds over Lake Superior \n(less over land, but still elevated), the ECMWF, Canadian, and GFS \nensembles agree that some form of elevated sustained winds, possibly \nup to gale-force, will occur. Soundings show a cold airmass well \nbelow 0C being advected in with deep mixing, reinforcing this \nnotion. Details of this event will continue to evolve. Ridging \nfollows into the weekend.",
